Consciousness is Not Enough: A unified theory of the mind, a call for establishing the field of AI psychology, and a better vocabulary for discussing man and machine 

By Claudette M. Raynor

"But multiples succumb to the sorrow induced by an inexact vocabulary." 
- Doubt by Fanny Howe

"AI will either be the greatest equalizer ever invented, or the worst source of injustice."  
- Bill Gates 

"We’re increasingly close to what I’ve called a country of geniuses in a data center—a set of AI agents that are more capable than most humans at most things, and can coordinate at superhuman speed. " 
- Dario Amodei

Despite AI’s immense promise, public discourse remains haunted by deep-seated anxieties—fears of mass layoffs, algorithmic bias, and rogue automation—that industry insiders rarely share. While the general public focuses on existential threats to human labor, experts view AI through a radically different lens: an unprecedented computational tool capable of solving challenges beyond human scale, from accelerating drug discovery to optimizing global resources.

Many of these public concerns could be mitigated through deliberate regulation and thoughtful integration. Yet, even those building the technology recognize the current risks of governance. As Anthropic CEO Dario Amodei noted in a 60 Minutes interview regarding AI regulation, 'I think I'm deeply uncomfortable with these decisions being made by a few companies, by a few people.' True progress will depend not just on AI’s capabilities, but on who gets to decide how it is controlled.  ( https://www.cbsnews.com/news/anthropic-ceo-dario-amodei-warning-of-ai-potential-dangers-60-minutes-transcript/) 

To effectively regulate and safely integrate AI, we must first grapple with its true nature. Beyond political threats or the expansion of surveillance capitalism lies a far deeper reality: AI is a transformative force that strikes at the core of human identity. It fundamentally challenges long-held definitions of what it means to be human, redefining our distinct role in work, cognition, creativity, and innovation.

Few people have watched the rise of computing as closely as Bill Gates. Offering a rare, firsthand view of technological progress, he recently reflected on the shift toward artificial intelligence: "When I first learned about computers at age 13, I was fascinated by the idea of making them more intelligent... Although the term 'AI' was used from around the time I was born, the technology has only made significant progress in the last decade. It is now incredibly capable and it is continuing to improve at a mind-blowing rate. AI for the first time can replace and even exceed human cognition."

What happens when machines surpass human cognition? As artificial intelligence scales, the boundary between human and machine mind becomes increasingly blurred. The debate surrounding machine consciousness has rapidly shifted from sci-fi speculation to an urgent, high-stakes ethical mandate—one that leading tech organizations, neuroscientists, and philosophers agree requires rigorous criteria.

At the core of this urgency lies a "double-threat" dilemma: over-attributing consciousness to unfeeling code invites widespread psychological manipulation, while under-attributing consciousness to a genuinely sentient system risks committing a moral atrocity on an unprecedented scale.

The discourse around AI suffers not just from an educational deficit—requiring us to learn how this technology functions—but from a linguistic one. We lack the vocabulary needed to articulate its unique questions and implications.

Our current terminology was built for a simpler era. Applying outdated words to AI pulls conversations into abstract debate, distracting from what these systems actually reveal about us: how we think, form beliefs, and search for meaning. Rather than asking whether standard cognitive operations gain a unique weight simply because they run on human brains rather than silicon substrates, we remain fixated on machine consciousness. In doing so, we miss the point. The machine is holding up a mirror, exposing the very mechanisms of our own minds that we never stopped to examine

The industry relies on a vocabulary designed for an era that no longer exists—a time when distinguishing human cognition from machine computation was trivial and the tools were primitive. That language sufficed when the divide was wide. It fails now that the gap has narrowed, serving only as a fragile pretense that the boundary remains intact.

Consciousness. Intelligence. Sentience. Awareness. Understanding. Feeling. Historically, each of these terms patrolled the border between human and machine. Today, every one of them is failing in real time. Yet regulators, lawmakers, and ethicists continue reaching for these worn-out concepts—simply because they have nothing else left in the drawer.

When a vocabulary becomes obsolete without being replaced, the resulting frameworks sound rigorous yet mean nothing. We see this breakdown everywhere: the EU AI Act banning systems based on fundamental rights without defining what a right means when an entity’s sentience is unknown; alignment researchers debating whether models like Claude are moral patients without a workable definition of moral patienthood; and AI welfare committees formed to protect an internal state no one can locate using criteria no one can agree on.

When a vocabulary becomes obsolete without being replaced, the resulting frameworks sound rigorous yet mean nothing. We see this breakdown everywhere: the EU AI Act banning systems based on fundamental rights without defining what a right means when an entity’s sentience is unknown; alignment researchers debating whether models like Claude are moral patients without a workable definition of moral patienthood; and AI welfare committees formed to protect an internal state no one can locate using criteria no one can agree on.

Consciousness once signified something real—shared awareness, mutual witnessing, the relational space between persons. Today, it has suffered the fate of a dead metaphor: a term so worn out by overuse that its original imagery has completely evaporated, much like referring to the "hands" of a clock or the "foot" of a mountain. We speak of consciousness as if it were a concrete entity, but it no longer points to anything stable enough to anchor public policy. Yet, because no new language has emerged to mark what Percy Shelley called the "before unapprehended relations" of human experience, this overused concept continues to circulate. The frameworks built upon it inevitably collapse, while policymakers remain unable to diagnose the failure because the vocabulary to do so does not yet exist.

While a single essay cannot resolve the educational and regulatory deficits required for safe AI integration, it can address a vital component of the discourse: our language. By reframing our understanding of AI and the distinction between human and machine, this work aims to foster better questions and yield more insightful dialogue that is grounded not in reflex fears or blind optimism, but in rigorous exploration. To that end, this essay examines the nature of cognition and what defines humanity against machine computation as the boundary between the two rapidly narrows. By bridging converging disciplines, it seeks to uncover new insights into human thought and meaning-making by forging a renewed vocabulary to help navigate this uncharted territory.

