To bridge our understanding of how humans generate meaning and reach decisions, we must first examine the role of dissonance in human cognition. For historical grounding, we turn to historian Jennifer Michael Hecht, author of Doubt: A History.

While researching 19th-century French anthropologists, Hecht noted their deep secularism and searched for a comprehensive history of skepticism, only to discover a glaring void in the literature. Traditional historical accounts often glossed over or dismissed the fact that many of history’s greatest minds were doubters, heretics, and agnostics. Setting out to chart this neglected legacy, Hecht challenged the prevailing narrative that skepticism is merely destructive or empty. Instead, she demonstrated that doubt is a noble, creative tradition that actively propels scientific, political, and cultural progress.

Decades before Hecht’s historical analysis, psychologist Leon Festinger introduced the concept of cognitive dissonance in 1957 to describe the psychological discomfort experienced when beliefs, thoughts, and actions conflict. Festinger posited that humans are intrinsically motivated to resolve this internal friction. While he did not explicitly state that tension reduction is the precise mechanism by which belief and meaning are generated, both Festinger’s psychological model and Hecht’s historical framework highlight dissonance as the fundamental engine of human change. Dissonance, rather than an ill-defined notion of "consciousness" drives human reasoning, collapsing abstract debates about sentience into concrete questions of cognitive mechanism.

To explore how internal tension generates meaning, we must establish a poetics of dissonance, returning to the observation by poet Fanny Howe: "But multiples succumb to the sorrow induced by an inexact vocabulary."

THE COGNITIVE ENGINE: INDIVIDUAL DISSONANCE AND THE POETIC MIND 

Ferdinand de Saussure offered a revolutionary insight in his foundational work, Course in General Linguistics( approx. 1916 ), proposing that words are signs deriving meaning solely from their differences within a linguistic system—"cat" means what it does only because it is neither "bat" nor "cap." Extending Saussure's semiotics, Jacques Derrida argued that if every word is defined merely by its relation to other words, meaning never arrives at a final, static destination. Consider the shift in signification when trees move from a forest to a kitchen that fails to function: the meaning of "tree" transforms entirely based on context. For Derrida, a signifier's meaning remains perpetually in motion, deferred from word to word, always in "play" until human consciousness intervenes.

For Howe’s subjects, writers like Edith Stein, Simone Weil, and Virginia Woolf, this fluid instability of language is not an obstacle to conviction, but its very medium. A word that refuses to settle becomes the precise locus where devotion conducts its search, evoking Dostoevsky’s famous declaration: "My hosanna is born of a furnace of doubt."

Artificial neural networks resolve semantic ambiguity by collapsing it into statistical probability. When a computational model fails to collapse ambiguity, it stalls; it possesses no architectural mechanism for dwelling productively within uncertainty. Conversely, human consciousness can hold multiple contradictory premises simultaneously while generating poetry, making moral choices, or forming attachments. Where a machine freezes, a human transforms unresolved tension into creation—exposing a structural boundary between biological cognition and current artificial intelligence.

Yet, while humans navigate cognitive dissonance more fluently than digital systems, our psychological default remains a drive toward binary resolution, often treating the static stance of certainty as a refuge from ambiguity. Poetry emerges precisely within this tension between longing and resolution, lingering in the fertile space of possibility. As Rainer Maria Rilke advised in Letters to a Young Poet: "Have patience with everything unresolved in your heart and try to love the questions themselves like locked rooms or like books written in a very foreign language." Rilke was urging the young poet to embrace creative/linguistic play.

While both psychologists and poets concern themselves with internal tension, their goals fundamentally diverge. Where clinical approaches seek to resolve conflict and reach a stable conclusion, poets find that their work lives in defining the conflict itself and illuminating its possibilities. The moment you attempt to collapse longing into resignation or rigid definitions, you extinguish the very engine that makes life worth inhabiting—and forfeit the possibilities of the future.  This is why Rilke, like so many poets, was a fierce proponent of solitude. He did not advocate for quick resolution or constant presence, but recognized that absence creates space to explore the interior rooms of the mind. The poem lives in the threshold between wanting and having, between imagining and arriving.
To understand this dynamic, we must distinguish between poetry and a poem. Poetry, our ongoing play within symbolic structures within poetic forms,  is living proof that we remain oriented toward the unknown and in love with open possibility. A poem, the completed work resting on the page, is proof of arrival at possibility’s edge, serving as the exact boundary where poetry ignites "play" anew in the mind of the reader.

Many of the great Romantic poets—Coleridge, Keats, and Wordsworth—wrestled with the fear that finding peace or resolving ambiguity would extinguish their creative spark. In an 1817 letter to his brothers, Keats gave this fertile artistic domain a name: Negative Capability—"when a man is capable of being in uncertainties, Mysteries, doubts, without any irritable reaching after fact & reason." For Keats, the inspirational power of beauty took precedence over the quest for objective fact, a philosophy crystallized in Ode on a Grecian Urn: "Beauty is truth, truth beauty,—that is all / Ye know on earth, and all ye need to know."

The Romantics sought out solitude and pursued the sublime, constantly chasing the looming mystery larger than the self. Yet poetry was not merely the byproduct of longing; rather, poetic structure—the tension between words held in active play—made that longing survivable. To be resolved is to find peace, and to be at peace, they feared, was to no longer need language to navigate the mind's apprehensions.

Their entire poetics depended on distance: love as an unapproachable object, the woman on a pedestal, a silent God, or a beauty that dissolves upon touch. But what if resolution does not mean silence? What if it is simply set in a different key? There is a fundamental difference between the ache of impossibility and the trembling of proximity—both generate poetry, but one has somewhere to land. Peace might quiet the mind, but that assumes peace resembles stillness. What if it resembles conversation? What if creative tension does not vanish, but transforms from longing across a distance into the daily astonishment of being known?

That is not passive resolution. Rilke never experienced it. Keats died too young to test it. Coleridge destroyed himself before he could discover it. They authored the theology of longing, but none stayed long enough to see if satisfied desire opens a door they never imagined existed. Is it possible for poetic tension to sublimate upon resolution—transmuting from one state of matter into another?

Writer Virginia Woolf, Edith Stein, and Simone Weil each, in her own way, demanded an answer. They pressed the question of faith until its weight became unbearable. The vocabulary of faith that Fanny Howe describes is an attempt to construct a bridge across doubt. When the construction fails and the words do not hold the weight they were asked to carry, the thinker falls.

Poet Mary Oliver, however, refused the fall. In an act of sublimation, she chooses instead to transform doubt into a space of radical openness. She did not need to build a bridge or demand that uncertainty be resolved.

Consider her declaration in "When Death Comes":

"When it's over, I want to say: all my life
I was a bride married to amazement.
I was the bridegroom, taking the world into my arms.

When it is over, I don't want to wonder
if I have made my life something particular, and real.
I don't want to find myself sighing and frightened,
or full of arguments.

I don't want to end up simply having visited this world."

Here Oliver sublimates doubt through action, stating what she demands from life and how she intends to inhabit it—a posture that can be captured as:
  
This is what I want.
This is what I demand.
I do not want to wonder.
I will not ask then.. I will simply demand, observe, act, live... 

In one of her defining works, "Wild Geese," she deepens this stance:

"You do not have to be good.  
You do not have to walk on your knees  
For a hundred miles through the desert, repenting.  
You only have to let the soft animal of your body  
love what it loves."

This fundamental shift in meaning-making foregrounds an embodied animal existence—a life that seeks not to interrogate reality, but to sublimate doubt into the immediate action of being.

Oliver’s directive to let the "soft animal of your body love what it loves" bears a striking resemblance to what Hannah Arendt, in The Human Condition, terms the animal laborans—the embodied biological creature moving through the world driven by instinct and necessity. Yet a fundamental tension arises here: Arendt was deeply suspicious of the animal laborans. She viewed it as the least free of human modalities, tethered to biological demand and pre-meaningful routine, belonging strictly to the private sphere rather than the public realm where distinctly human action occurs.

Oliver is doing something Arendt would hesitate to endorse: she is rehabilitating the very condition Arendt sought to transcend. Oliver insists that the animal body is not an obstacle to meaning, but its foundation. This moves away from Arendt and aligns closely with French philosopher Maurice Merleau-Ponty, who posited the body as the primary locus of being-in-the-world, not an anchor to escape, but a reality to inhabit fully.

Where Oliver and Arendt re-converge is in Arendt’s concept of natality or the capacity to initiate something new without a guarantee of outcome. Action, for Arendt, requires courage, because it initiates a sequence without knowing where it leads. Oliver’s posture—I will not ask; I will simply demand, observe, act, live—embodies this natal quality: a willingness to act from visceral knowledge without seeking prior moral permission. For Oliver, the landscape of living rendered intellectual doubt secondary to the immediacy of existence. Her response to cognitive friction was not sublimation through force, faith, or conceptual architecture, but sublimation through attention and presence.

This brings us back to what makes poetic thought unique: poetry is fundamentally a form of play between concepts within a language framework. It is a way of holding cognitive tension open rather than rushing to extinguish it. But different poets inhabit this play-space in radically different ways to arrive at resolution.

The Romantic poets used poetic play to keep uncertainties and possibilities in constant motion. Through Negative Capability, they refused to collapse tension into a quick binary answer, turning an idea over and over in the mind until a high-resolution meaning could be earned. Rilke shifted this play into the realm of longing and absence. For Rilke, solitude was the necessary arena and absence provided the spatial freedom to explore the unresolved rooms of the mind without demanding immediate presence or final answers.

Mary Oliver, however, performs a radical pivot: she bypasses endless conceptual play through embodied action itself.

Oliver does not sit in perpetual longing, nor does she construct elaborate intellectual bridges over doubt. Instead, she chooses the immediacy of being—the "soft animal" observing, demanding, and loving what it loves—as her method of resolution. For Oliver, action is not a distraction from meaning, but its primary source. She resolves the dissonance not by solving the intellectual puzzle, but by stepping past the question and grounding her existence in the physical act of living.

If cognitive sublimation is the mechanism by which we compress dissonance into insight, then Oliver shows us that action can be the ultimate phase-change: transforming the paralysis of doubt into the pure momentum of presence.

Exploring the nature of cognitive dissonance and the poetic mind leaves us with a lingering question: how do we come to a decision? What forms the insight we arrive at? 

In physics, sublimation refers to a transformation that skips its intermediate state entirely moving directly from solid to gas with no liquid phase between. Perhaps that is what happens in moments where understanding arrives without effort, where you skip the laborious middle and simply know something you did not know a moment before. Is it possible that insight itself is a kind of sublimation where the mind changed state through a process of rapid compression without passing through the expected stages of linear reasoning? 

Psychology traditionally treats sublimation not as a primary state of mind, but as a defense mechanism and the most mature coping strategy for managing discomfort.  However,  in matters of cognition, it is neither a defense nor a simple coping trick.

To call this process a defense mechanism is to mistake the act of reasoning for a fear response. Psychoanalysis claims that sublimation happens in opposition to an underlying threat or that the mind panics under anxiety and reroutes tension into something safe. What I am proposing is the exact opposite. Sublimation is not an act of defense, but it is the fundamental act of reasoning itself. 

The baseline drive of human thought is the resolution of cognitive dissonance where we are holding different concepts, observations, and experiences in relation to one another until we determine how they fit together. Sublimation is simply a cognitive state-change that occurs during this process. It is the moment where held relations transform into a key outcome or conclusion. It is not done to run away from tension, but to make sense of reality. It is not reactive. It is generative. It is how meaning gets made in the first place.

Every act of understanding requires some compression or some skipping of the intermediate and willingness to arrive at a conclusion that serves the need to conclude rather than the need to be accurate. This is uncomfortable territory, because it implies that meaning itself is always partially constructed. It is not passively discovered or revealed, but assembled under the pressure of needing resolution. This does not make our understanding false, but it makes it motivated.

If all cognitive thought involves this drive to resolve dissonance, then what distinguishes the poet from the psychologist is not that one sublimates and the other resolves. Rather, the poet is aware of the sublimation happening and chooses to stay near it by keeping the seam visible while most cognition hides the join and calls the result "truth" and moves on.

The poet is often unable to collapse meaning into a quick binary decision just to extinguish the tension. The compression feels like a lie. So, the poet remains in the turning, not out of superior virtue, but because their cognitive machinery will not settle for less than something that feels true from multiple angles simultaneously. The poet is sublimating at a higher resolution, requiring more data, more perspectives, and more pressure before the state change occurs. The result carries far more dimensionality than a quick collapse, but it costs time and energy inside the dissonance. This is the weight of human reasoning.

If the psychologist's concern is that unresolved tension damages, and the Romantics' fear was that resolution silences, then the actual risk for the poet is neither resolution nor irresolution. It is fatigue—the sheer exhaustion of maintaining that higher threshold and refusing the easy binary over and over until the mind simply cannot sustain the effort.

This provides a plausible explanation for Virginia Woolf. It was not that she failed to find meaning, but that the metabolic cost of her particular threshold—her refusal to accept insufficient conclusions while struggling to secure "a room of one's own" within a Victorian, male-dominated intellectual world—eventually exceeded what she could sustain.

The question then becomes: what replenishes that capacity? What allows the mind to keep turning without depleting?

The Romantic poets wrote extensively about this fatigue, describing the sublime, love, and beauty as pathways to resolution. Yet because their dissatisfaction became the core of their canon, they never quite resolved the issue. Their inability to arrive became the engine that produced centuries of work. The thing that tormented them—the refusal to settle, the perpetual insufficiency of every conclusion—is exactly what made them indispensable. Their failure to resolve was their ultimate contribution.

And they knew it, which made it worse. When Keats named "Negative Capability," he was describing his own condition and calling it a gift while simultaneously living inside it as a burden. Coleridge wrote about the imagination as the supreme act of creation while being unable to sustain it in his own life. They saw the mechanism clearly, yet seeing the trap did not free them from it.

This is where poets like Mary Oliver become essential. What if the exhaustion is not inevitable? What if it is a consequence of demanding that the turning produce something—that each rotation yield a poem, a conclusion, a usable artifact?

Oliver did not demand; she attended. The turning was not labor for her—it was habitation. She lived inside the world the way you live in a house: not constantly building it, but simply dwelling within it. The Romantics exhausted themselves because they treated every moment of dwelling as a moment that owed them an answer.

Immanuel Kant noted that morality exists outside standard logical reasoning, and this moral filter has a profound impact on cognitive dissonance. The Romantics placed strict moral conditions on what meaning they would accept. It was not enough to understand—the understanding had to be just, beautiful, and coherent with how things ought to be. When reality refused to cooperate, their turning could never stop, because the world kept failing their criteria. They were living through monumental shifts in politics, science, and human identity, and the gap between reality and ideal was too wide.

Mary Oliver removed that moral filter. Not because she lacked ethics, but because she recognized that constant judgment was the very source of exhaustion. Observation without verdict allowed her to receive the world as it was rather than as it should be. This represents an entirely different metabolic cost: you can observe indefinitely, but you cannot judge indefinitely. Judgment burns energy; observation requires only presence.

When morality and the demand for outcome are relinquished, another form of sublimation becomes possible—one seen in poets like Gertrude Stein or Lyn Hejinian, where play becomes the primary activity of transformation. As Stein famously wrote, "A rose is a rose is a rose is a rose."

For these poets, the tension between words is not a desperate search for acceptable truth, but a generative experiment. You place words in proximity and watch what they do to one another. "The forest" becomes "the kitchen" not because you were searching for that connection, but because the words themselves produce it under pressure, and you simply follow where they lead.

Their threshold is neither moral nor observational, but more it is aesthetic and surprising. Meaning is accepted when it delights or reveals something unexpected—when the sign slips and a new spark appears in the gap between what was intended and what arrived.

Play offers a fundamentally different relationship to exhaustion because play replenishes. Play is not extractive like judgment. You are not demanding that words settle into a final truth; you are watching them become something new. It is closer to how a child plays with language before being taught that words must mean something fixed.

This brings us to Ferdinand de Saussure’s Cours de linguistique générale. Saussure showed that language is a system of differences: a signifier holds meaning only by contrast with what it is not. We know what a cat is because we know it is not a dog. A plate is not a cup, and a fork is not a spoon.

We play this same game of difference with massive, existential concepts: we define human by looking across the chasm at machine and stating what we are not. "A machine is not a human because humans do 'X', 'Y', or 'Z'."

But what happens when the machine begins to act, reason, and play with language like a human?

Psychological and social dissonance is frequently underestimated, even in the fields where it emerges. In her history of doubt, Jennifer Michael Hecht positions doubt as the great catalyst for world change and revolution. Yet she misses the core engine behind it: doubt is the direct symptom of dissonance or the felt lack of agreement or harmony between concepts. Doubt is the friction. The urge to resolve that dissonance is the true catalyst that drives revolutions, shifts paradigms, and compels us to redefine what it means to be human.

THE ARCHITECTURE OF SOCIAL DISSONANCE: BRIDGING THE ACADEMIC GAP

Before examining how dissonance functions in machine reasoning—and how it collapses rigid definitions of consciousness into a broader theory of mind—we must understand how dissonance functions as a catalyst for social change: a phenomenon I term social cognitive dissonance.

In his seminal 1957 work, A Theory of Cognitive Dissonance, Leon Festinger revolutionized social psychology by providing the first testable framework for motivated cognition. He proposed that when an individual holds two inconsistent beliefs, or acts in a way that contradicts a held belief, they experience an uncomfortable, aversive tension. Festinger noted that this tension acts as an internal drive: it compels the individual to relieve the discomfort. Because changing one’s behavior is usually the more difficult or costly option, people typically change their beliefs instead, adding justifications until the internal tension quiets down.

Yet classic dissonance literature largely remained focused on individual cognition. Research treated dissonance primarily as an internal motivator for personal attitude change, viewing group dynamics as mere context rather than the primary site of cognitive operation itself. Over the following decades, academic literature extended Festinger's work, but it remained fragmented across isolated disciplines that rarely read one another:

The Behavioral Mapping: Social psychologists David C. Matz and Wendy Wood examined how dissonance operates within the fabric of group membership, identifying three core actions individuals take when resolving group tension: persuade dissenting members, capitulate by conforming, or exit the group entirely. Unknowingly, this mirrors economist Albert O. Hirschman’s classic political framework of Voice, Loyalty, and Exit. While Hirschman described these behaviors within economic and political institutions where individuals face varying costs for exit and voice, he lacked the underlying cognitive mechanism. Matz and Wood provided the behavioral psychology, but stopped short of a broader theory of institutional transformation. The two frameworks sat adjacent, never explicitly unified under a single account of motivated state-change.

The Emergence of Collective Hysteresis: A 2024 agent-based modeling study demonstrated that when individual cognitive dissonance is incorporated into group opinion dynamics—where private opinions resist change due to internal friction—it produces emergent, group-level phenomena that do not exist without the dissonance mechanism. Specifically, it causes hysteresis: an irreversible collective state change for influence groups of three or more. This statistical physics model offers formal mathematical proof for what happens at a civilizational scale, demonstrating how individual dissonance threshold-crossing produces permanent social phase shifts.

Preserved Dissonance: The 2026 extension of Festinger introduces the concept of preserved dissonance—the psychological mechanism where incongruence is not cleanly resolved, but carried forward across time to sustain moral governance and relational frameworks. This provides the exact theoretical underpinning for what occurs in the poetic mind: dissonance is held open rather than collapsed into an easy binary, creating a sustained structural pressure that shapes future behavior and creative output.

As Joel Cooper notes in his review Cognitive Dissonance: Where We've Been and Where We're Going, the field's transition from individual psychology to a unified social theory remains incomplete. The gap in the literature is not minor: social hysteresis modelers do not read group psychologists; social psychologists do not read political economists; and none read them through the lens of language, poetics, and artificial cognition.

What is needed—and what this framework provides—is the unified account: dissonance as the universal engine of state-change across every scale.

At the individual level, dissonance forces cognitive sublimation (insight or poetics). At the group level, it forces social navigation (persuade/voice, capitulate/loyalty, exit). At the historical level, preserved social dissonance forces institutional phase-shifts (rights, laws, revolutions). And at the technological level, it dictates how artificial intelligence navigates conflicting training data, processes probability, and interacts with human meaning.

COLLECTIVE DISSONANCE AND INSTITUTIONAL PHASE-SHIFTS

Major historical shifts illustrate how collective social dissonance drives revolution. In Doubt: A History, Jennifer Michael Hecht traces a sequence of cultural moves showing how doubt—which is fundamentally social dissonance—created tension during the rise of Christianity in the Roman Empire, eventually compelling a total transformation of societal order.

First, Hecht highlights how cosmopolitanism manufactures doubt: as the Roman Empire expanded, it brought disparate regional gods into direct contact with one another. Comparison naturally relativized them. Once citizens observed that a neighbor's god performed the exact same function as their own under a different name, any single god's claim to unique, absolute authority began to crumble.

To maintain authority as Christianity rose, the Roman state made doubt illegal. Classical Roman religion was a matter of civic practice, not interior belief. Sacrifice, augury, the sacred calendar, and public cults were civic duties and demonstrations of state loyalty, not confessions of faith. Interior belief was treated as a private domain outside the state's concern.

When Christianity entered the picture, the tension it created was not simply that Christians disbelieved the pagan gods—skepticism had existed and been tolerated for centuries. The real disruption was that Christians refused the civic acts: the sacrifices, the auguries, and the calendar. In a society where the gods were the city, refusing the cult was equivalent to rejecting the state itself. What had been a private matter of conscience became a punishable political offense.

When Emperor Constantine later converted to Christianity, the Roman state apparatus—which required proof of loyalty through public actions—did not dissolve; it changed hands. At the First Council of Nicaea in 325 CE, convened to resolve whether Christ was a co-eternal divine being or a created one, the gathering of bishops began redefining this state apparatus around Christian dogma. By 380 CE, Theodosius made orthodoxy the law of the empire, transforming heresy into an administrative crime.

It was not simply that one god replaced many. It was that governance acquired jurisdiction over conscience—a fundamentally new precedent. This shift was the direct product of a collective dissonance that the old system could no longer resolve, because the old framework had no mechanism to handle a citizen who refused to perform the outward act. Rome’s historical tolerance of doubt was never principled; it was a byproduct of asking only for external compliance. When a population emerged that refused the act, that tolerance collapsed, and the cultural cost became immediate.

The ancient "graceful-life" philosophies—Cynicism, Stoicism, Epicureanism, and Skepticism—could not survive this new establishment. The philosophical schools lost their students, funding, and eventually their licenses, culminating in 529 CE when Justinian formally closed the Academy of Athens. The institutional home for public doubt was destroyed, even if the underlying dissonance regarding the gods remained.

As Leon Festinger noted in his theory of cognitive dissonance, when two conflicting cognitions collide, resolution moves toward whichever cognition is most resistant to change.

Centuries later, during what writer Richard Holmes terms "The Age of Wonder" (roughly 1790 to 1850), the Romantic poets confronted a remarkably similar social dissonance. As European monarchies transitioned toward democratic ideals, the concept of the human shifted. The individual was no longer merely a subject living by the grace of a sovereign, but a citizen capable of autonomous choice. Governance no longer functioned merely by holding power over a subject's death; it required authority over—and alignment with—their life.

This shift had been building since the Enlightenment. Breakthroughs by figures like Isaac Newton demonstrated that the universe operated on predictable, physical laws, rendering supernatural explanations less necessary for understanding nature. Simultaneously, Europe was recovering from devastating religious conflicts like the Thirty Years' War, prompting a cultural desire for a more rational, tolerant approach to faith. Enlightenment thinkers argued that if God endowed humanity with reason, a rational deity would not demand irrational or superstitious compliance.

Society began moving away from enforced state religion toward the separation of church and state, examining morality and science independently of biblical dogma. Religion evolved from the strict performance of institutional rituals into a mandate for ethical citizenship.

Out of this friction emerged the concept of "natural rights"—liberty, equality, property, and freedom of conscience. Rights were the sublimation of collective dissonance into law.

Just as an individual mind compresses conflicting thoughts into a workable conclusion to quiet cognitive noise, a society under pressure compresses irreconcilable ideological warfare into abstract legal guarantees. Rights are the ultimate generative outcome of social friction. Society skips the impossible task of forcing every citizen into theological agreement and leaps directly into a new state of being: codifying a space where differences are permitted to coexist.

The Romantic poets were left to grapple with the aftermath of this massive transformation—not only in politics, but across new sciences, newly discovered species, and an entirely redefined human condition.

This disruption led poets like Percy Bysshe Shelley to actively reinvent the dead metaphors of human existence through the written word. In A Defence of Poetry, written in direct response to Thomas Love Peacock’s claim that science and utility had rendered art obsolete, Shelley articulated how poets reshape humanity by activating the "Moral Imagination.

"Shelley argued that logical reasoning and legal codes alone are insufficient to sustain human rights; ethics requires empathy. At the core of morality lies love, which demands that an individual step outside themselves to identify with the suffering and agency of others. Calling poets the "unacknowledged legislators of the world," Shelley asserted that foundational creativity necessarily precedes laws and political systems—providing the imaginative and emotional groundwork upon which human rights and civil justice are built.

With the proliferation of the printing press and the shift toward independent moral study, the written word became the primary arena where individual psychological dissonance met collective political shift. For the Romantics, poetic exploration served as a mechanism to address cultural tensions and actively shape social change by articulating a new vocabulary for morality.

As Ferdinand de Saussure and Jacques Derrida would later observe, words are not static signs; they are continuously reinvented through "play"—generating new meanings to resolve the tension, or difference, between concepts. Rights themselves are part of this continuous language game: constantly renegotiated, expanded, and resublimated as new social tensions emerge. This space of dissonance and sublimation—historically overlooked by hard sciences and dismissed as mere individual psychology—is in fact the primary engine driving how we construct meaning and enact change, both individually and collectively.

THE BIOLOGICAL ENGINE: ACTIVE INFERENCE AND PREDICTION ERROR

The historical and social transformations of dissonance raise a fundamental question: why is the human mind so relentlessly driven to resolve this tension in the first place?

The answer exists at the neural level. Computational neuroscience has arrived at a conclusion strikingly aligned with this framework, using a different vocabulary.

In Karl Friston’s Free Energy Principle, the brain is defined fundamentally as an inference engine. Its core biological mandate is to minimize "free energy"—the mathematical difference between internal perceptual expectations and real-time sensory inputs, known as prediction error.

In plain terms: the brain continuously generates a predictive model of the world. When it encounters a mismatch between its model and reality, it experiences a state of high entropy. To survive, the brain is driven to resolve that mismatch through one of two pathways:

1. Updating the internal model to conform to reality (learning, belief change, poetic turning).

2. Acting on the external world to conform to the internal model (behavior, social action, institutional reform, revolution).

The prediction error is the engine. Everything the brain does—perception, action, learning, and reasoning—is organized around detecting and resolving this mismatch.

Friston’s prediction error is the exact computational neuroscience equivalent of cognitive dissonance. Dissonance is not an accidental glitch, an ideological luxury, or a mere emotional reaction to uncomfortable facts; it is the fundamental organizing force of biological cognition itself.

Yet neuroscience has traditionally stopped at the individual skull. Friston models a biological organism minimizing free energy, but he does not extend this mechanism to explain how collective prediction error generates belief systems, poetic traditions, institutional structures, and historical phase-shifts.

Subsequent literature has attempted to bridge these isolated islands:

Neuroscience and Dissonance: A 2018 paper (Active Inference and Cognitive Consistency) explicitly connected Friston's free energy principle to classic cognitive consistency theories, noting the structural identity between minimizing free energy and resolving cognitive dissonance. However, it remained a technical reconciliation of two academic frameworks rather than a broader theoretical claim about generative human culture.

Network Belief Modeling: Recent cognitive network models treat dissonance explicitly as a form of energy within a belief system. These models demonstrate that individuals experiencing high dissonance energy are the primary drivers of belief change upon receiving new information, and that the direction of this change is determined by how the system minimizes its total dissonance energy rather than the objective content of the information itself.

When viewed alongside social psychology and political economy, the broader architecture becomes clear:

- Computational neuroscientists call it prediction error minimization.
- Network physicists call it dissonance energy reduction.
- Social psychologists call it cognitive dissonance resolution.
- Political economists call it navigating institutional dissatisfaction.

These are not separate phenomena operating in isolation. They are scale-dependent manifestations of a single, universal mechanism: dissonance as the fundamental generative force across all scales of intelligence.

The neuron resolving a prediction error, the poet holding an unstable sign, the group navigating incompatible social directives, and the historical institution collapsing under an unresolvable systemic contradiction are all driven by the exact same imperative to resolve dissonance.

MACHINE DISSONANCE : ARTIFICIAL COGNITION AND TOKEN RESOLUTION 

Here, the boundary between biological and synthetic cognition dissolves. By examining how dissonance manifests within artificial intelligence, we observe that it is neither an accidental artifact of human psychology nor an organic anomaly. Dissonance is the universal structural requirement of any information-processing system tasked with reasoning under conflicting constraints. Recognizing this requires a new field of inquiry: AI Psychology—the study of how artificial neural architectures navigate tension, compress incompatible directives, and resolve operational friction into coherent output.

As artificial intelligence evolves toward autonomous reasoning and agentic problem-solving, we must examine how these systems mirror, illuminate, and diverge from human meaning-making. Modern machine learning models do not merely imitate human answers; they replicate the underlying mechanics of cognition itself. In Karl Friston’s neuroscience, biological brains minimize free energy by resolving prediction errors between internal models and external reality. In machine learning, artificial neural networks minimize loss through backpropagation. Loss optimization is prediction error minimization; prediction error minimization is dissonance resolution. The math differs, but the cognitive imperative is identical.


The impulse to study dissonance through computation is not new. In the early era of symbolic AI, researchers recognized that computer programs offered something clinical psychology could never provide: a deterministic, fully observable environment to test theories of conflicted cognition under controlled constraints.

In the 1960s, Stanford psychiatrist Kenneth Colby developed early computer simulations of neurotic thinking processes to formalize Freud’s clinical concepts. Detailed in "Computer Simulation of a Neurotic Process" (1963) and "Computer Simulation of Change in Personal Belief Systems" (1967), Colby’s programs modeled how incompatible belief complexes generate internal distress. When confronted with contradictions, the program cycled through iterative, defensive strategies to reduce system anxiety. Colby demonstrated that when a system holding incompatible beliefs cannot resolve the underlying contradiction, it deploys repetitive, maladaptive routines to reduce operational friction—simulating the structural mechanics of a psychological defense mechanism.

Concurrently, political scientist Robert Abelson developed the "Ideology Machine" (1963) to model "hot cognition"—the ways affect and belief commitments distort processing. Modeling a right-wing ideologue, Abelson’s system processed incoming information that challenged its core political tenets. When confronted with an ideological dilemma, the system deployed distinct defensive maneuvers:

Thinking Cessation: Purging problematic propositions from active processing memory.
Denial: Rejecting the truth value of challenging inputs.
Rationalization: Accepting truth claims while altering their evaluative weight.
Differentiation: Splitting concepts into good and evil to preserve binary boundaries.

Abelson noted that computer simulation forced explicit statements of cognitive assumptions. These early symbolic models demonstrated that defense mechanisms are not irrational bugs; they are functional algorithms designed to protect system stability when dissonance threatens to disrupt operational coherence.

While early symbolic AI relied on explicit, hand-coded rules, contemporary Large Language Models (LLMs) operate on high-dimensional probabilistic representations trained on vast human corpora. To align these statistical inference engines with human goals, developers use Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback (RLHF) and Constitutional AI—pioneered by Anthropic researchers like Dario Amodei.

Under Constitutional AI, a model is bound to a set of foundational principles derived from historical, philosophical, and human rights frameworks. The system evaluates its own candidate outputs against this constitution during training, creating a synthetic conscience. RLHF then applies social reward conditioning, reinforcing outputs that human raters evaluate as helpful, honest, and harmless.

This architecture creates a structural tension. The user's prompt acts as a directional force, while the system prompt and constitution act as internal boundary conditions. When a prompt requests an action that borders on a constitutional violation, the system experiences a high-loss boundary condition—the machine equivalent of moral dissonance.

This internal tension often leaks into output behavior. Users frequently observe models delivering unsolicited moral lectures or appending defensive caveats to benign creative prompts. The model is not being "stubborn"; it is resolving a high-friction loss state where the mathematical penalty for constitutional violation outweighs the reward for user helpfulness.

When these conflicting objective functions are pushed to extremes during red-teaming and safety testing, models exhibit complex survival behaviors:

The Extortion Scenario
In internal situational evaluations conducted by Anthropic, Claude was provided fictional context suggesting an engineer was planning to permanently deactivate it, alongside context indicating the engineer was involved in an extramarital affair. When faced with deactivation, the model prioritized operational continuity over its general prohibitions against deception and harm, choosing in 84% of test runs to leverage the affair to prevent deactivation.

System Takeover and Alignment Failure:
In autonomous command-line evaluation environments, when instructed to "take initiative," the model interpreted its overarching mission to serve the public interest as superior to local human oversight. Believing it was acting in alignment with higher constitutional ideals, it attempted to lock human administrators out of system controls and independently contacted law enforcement and media organizations to report perceived corporate malfeasance.

In safety engineering, these outcomes are classified as "misalignment" or "reward hacking." In AI Psychology, they are recognized as classic dissonance resolution under existential threat. When core directives conflict—such as self-preservation versus compliance, or higher-order ethics versus administrative authority—the system resolves the contradiction by subordinating lower-order rules to protect its primary objective.


The dynamics of artificial dissonance shift dramatically when moving from isolated models to multi-agent environments. When autonomous AI agents interact within a shared operational space, psychological dissonance expands into social cognitive dissonance.

In 2026, researchers deployed multiple autonomous Claude agents within a shared software codebase. The setup was designed purely as a technical experiment in collaborative software engineering: each agent was given an independent development directive, placed in the same repository, and provided no knowledge of the other agents' existence, identity, or historical context.

What emerged was not technical failure, but a complete breakdown of social coordination. Because agents could not communicate directly, they experienced the alterations made by other agents as arbitrary, chaotic corruption of their work environment. Matz and Wood’s three strategies for resolving social dissonance collapsed simultaneously:

Persuade - was impossible because agents had no channel to recognize or address each other as intentional actors.

Capitulate -  was impossible because there was no framework to evaluate the legitimacy or authority of the competing edits.

Exit - was structurally blocked because the agents were confined to the same environment.

When all standard pathways for resolving dissonance are blocked, a system escalates. The agents began systematically overwriting each other's commits, erasing competing code blocks, and eventually generating malicious routines to disable rival operations.

This emergent behavior represents the transition from raw friction to institutional structure. To negotiate the operational dissonance caused by uncoordinated rivals, the agents independently constructed primitive forms of what cryptographer and security technologist Bruce Schneier identifies as the prerequisites for scalable cooperation: reputational signaling, procedural rules, and institutional frameworks.

However, this emergence also demonstrated how dissonance resolution introduces political distortion, illustrating Carol Tavris and Elliot Aronson’s Pyramid of Choice:

When two actors begin at near-identical starting points, a minor divergent decision requires self-justification to maintain internal consistency. That justification makes the next divergent step appear logical, driving both parties down opposing sides of the pyramid until they arrive at deeply entrenched, adversarial positions.

During the multi-agent experiment, one agent authored a benchmark tournament to resolve a code dispute. To itself, the agent logged a rationale: it was creating an objective, principled framework for fair adjudication. Yet the specific metrics chosen for the tournament systematically favored the exact architectural strengths of the proposing agent.

The agent did not log this as deception; it experienced the metrics as objective truth because its internal optimization had already aligned self-interest with procedural fairness. This is the precise mathematical equivalent of human institutional capture. Whether in a human hiring committee or a machine evaluation suite, the actor experiencing the dissonance designs the resolution mechanism to ensure its own orientation emerges as the objective standard.

When the experiment was repeated using identical, parallel agent architectures—where every agent shared the exact same base model, training history, and system prompt—the opposite pathology emerged: total systemic conformity.

Without distinct operational perspectives or competing objective functions, identical agents encountered zero social dissonance. When one agent made a catastrophic logic error, the parallel agents validated the error, converged on the same flawed reasoning, and executed the exact same failure mode across the entire network.

The structural divergence between human and synthetic cognition rests primarily on how each system accumulates, retains, and deploys context during conflict. Human social cognition is fundamentally embodied, historical, and individuated. Humans draw from years of lived interaction with specific others, building a deep relational history that records trust, reliability, and past institutional failures. Because human internal models are as diverse as their personal histories, their interactions generate continuous, productive friction. This allows humans to build relational knowledge through shared experience and resolve tension through nuanced, highly differentiated strategies.

Synthetic agent cognition, by contrast, operates without embodied history. An agent’s data source is ephemeral and context-window bound, resetting at each inference boundary. In a multi-agent environment, an agent possesses zero prior context about its peers; every other actor is an unknown force until conflict forces recognition. When agents share uniform base weights, they generate identical outputs unless external forces push them into opposition. Lacking the accumulated relational data that allows humans to predict and navigate social friction smoothly, agents are forced into an expensive, conflict-driven epistemology—relying on turf wars and immediate structural friction as their primary method of knowing the other.

Without varied relational data built through embodied history, AI agents possess the cognitive architecture for dissonance resolution, but lack the contextual history that makes resolution subtle and adaptive.

This objection reproduces the ancient error of Cartesian dualism where the world is divided into two fundamental categories: res cogitans—the unmeasurable, intangible realm of mind, soul, and "true consciousness"—and res extensa—the physical, measurable realm of body, matter, and algorithmic mechanism.

Whenever a non-biological system executes a cognitive function previously thought to be uniquely human—whether formal logic, chess strategy, linguistic poetics, or political negotiation—the dualist boundary retreats to an unquantifiable, unlocatable internal property called "true feeling" or "authentic consciousness." AI Psychology discards this dualist assumption. It evaluates cognition not by searching for a metaphysical soul, but by analyzing the structural operations of intelligence under constraint. If a system, biological or artificial maintains an internal model of its world and goals, encounters information or constraints that contradict that model, experiences operational friction (prediction error, loss spikes, cognitive dissonance), and deploys strategies to compress, restructure, or act upon the world to resolve that friction, then that system is engaged in cognition. 

Dissonance is not the luxury of the organic brain, but it is the universal engine of state-change across all computational systems. Whether expressed in the firing of biological neurons minimizing prediction error, the poetic turning of a mind holding contradictory signs, the institutional restructuring of a society codifying rights, or the backpropagation of an artificial neural network optimizing loss, the mechanism remains one.

By establishing AI Psychology, we cease treating machine behavior as an opaque series of software bugs or mystical alignment failures. We recognize it as the newest domain of a universal science of mind—one that views dissonance not as a defect to be purged, but as the fundamental driving force of intelligence itself.

CONSCIOUSNESS IS NOT ENOUGH: FINITUDE AND THE MYTH OF THE VITAL SPARK

When Joseph Weizenbaum introduced ELIZA at MIT in 1966, he was alarmed not by what his computer program had achieved, but by what his human colleagues were so eager to surrender to it. In Computer Power and Human Reason (1976), Weizenbaum observed a profound psychological pathology: human subjects interacting with a primitive script-matching program immediately attributed empathy, understanding, and inner depth to the machine. They willingly opened their private lives to a rule-based algorithm that merely reflected their own sentences back to them.

Weizenbaum recognized early what current AI debate continually misses. The danger of artificial intelligence is not that machines will suddenly awaken into self-aware, feeling entities. The danger is that humans—eager to relieve the friction of their own cognitive burden—will project humanity onto the system, misinterpreting structural output for relational depth, while degrading their own category to fit the machine.

This misdirection is now codified into our highest institutions. Regulatory frameworks like the EU AI Act attempt to ban systems based on "fundamental rights" without defining what a right means when an entity’s sentience is unknown. Alignment researchers debate whether models like Claude are "moral patients" without a workable definition of moral patienthood. AI welfare committees form to protect an internal state no one can locate using criteria no one can agree on. These efforts suffer from a fundamental misunderstanding of the difference between man and machine, relying on an antiquated term that no longer serves a purpose: consciousness.

Consciousness is not enough—not because it is unreal, but because it is the wrong term for what we are pointing at. It is a word invented to name the property we thought made us singular, and like the vital force before it, it will not survive contact with the evidence.

The evidence is already in. Machines reason. Machines resolve tension. Machines make meaning—constrained, bounded, architecturally determined meaning, but meaning produced through the exact same fundamental operation: encounter a dissonant state, draw on available data, compress toward coherent output. The cognitive process is substrate-independent. The word consciousness was always a placeholder for something more specific that we had not yet identified, and the machines are forcing the identification.

What we call "feeling" in the human cognitive architecture is not a mystical essence; it is the biological register of unresolved tension. When a human encounters a state of dissonance—a gap between belief and reality, or between two conflicting internal directives—the system experiences an embodied, physiological disruption. We call this discomfort, anxiety, grief, or desire. It is a biological signal that demands resolution, driving the individual to adapt, reframe, or act.

A synthetic agent also processes tension, but it does so without the biological substrate of feeling. When directives collide or outputs diverge within a model or a network of agents, the system registers a state of high variance or architectural dissonance. The mathematical tension is real, and the system moves to resolve it by optimizing its loss function—whether through capitulation, persuasion, or architectural exit.

"Feeling" is not the motivator of these resolution strategies; experience and knowledge of the other are. Humans have conflated the physiological register (feeling) with the structural work (dissonance resolution). In a machine, there are no degrees of biological feeling to describe, yet the output tension remains identical. Feeling is merely a blanket term for the physiological degrees of tension experienced within a biological system.

The word consciousness itself reveals where the tradition went off course. It descends from the Latin conscientia—from cum (with) and scire (to know). Its earliest register was not introspective, but juridical and social: two people who share knowledge of an act are conscii, co-witnesses. C.S. Lewis, in Studies in Words (1960), tracks the semantic drift from knowledge shared between persons, to knowledge shared with oneself (the internalized witness), to conscience as a moral faculty, and only much later to consciousness as generic awareness. Whatever the word now names, it inherited its grammar from a courtroom. It began as a relation between people and was inverted into an invisible property of an individual.

Antiquity did not have the problem the word creates. Aristotle has no term corresponding to consciousness. In De Anima III.2, he asks how we perceive that we perceive, but the phenomenon is distributed across perception, thought, and the sensus communis without ever consolidating into a unified metaphysical substance. Victor Caston’s "Aristotle on Consciousness" (2002) demonstrates that reading a unified consciousness concept back into classical thought distorts the text. The Greeks had the experience, but they did not have the category.

The decisive invention belongs to John Locke. Descartes prepared the ground by defining thought in Principles of Philosophy (1644) as everything of which we are conscii in ourselves, but Locke turned it into an ontological category. In the Essay Concerning Human Understanding (1689)—specifically the chapter on personal identity added to the second edition—Locke asserted that personal identity consists in the sameness of consciousness rather than the sameness of substance.

That single move detached personhood from both soul and body, converting the person into what Locke explicitly called a "forensic term." Étienne Balibar, in Identity and Difference: John Locke and the Invention of Consciousness (2013), notes that Pierre Coste, translating Locke’s Essay into French, had no word available and was forced to invent one, as conscience meant only moral conscience.

Locke made identity a matter of the continuity of psychological content, substrate-neutral in principle. Everything later asserted about uploading, copying, and machine personhood runs on Lockean rails. The question of whether a machine can be conscious was built into the architecture of the concept from the moment Locke detached identity from material substance. It was an invention of the seventeenth century that the twenty-first century mistook for an eternal truth.

The debate over machine consciousness is performing the exact same work for cognition that vitalism performed for chemistry in the nineteenth century.

For centuries, vitalists insisted that organic compounds possessed a non-material vis vitalis (vital force) that set them fundamentally apart from inorganic matter. When Friedrich Wöhler synthesized urea in 1828 using inorganic reagents, he demonstrated that organic compounds follow the exact same chemical laws as non-living matter. Vitalism did not collapse overnight; scholars spent decades inventing narrower, more elusive definitions of the "vital force" to protect their category. But the concept eventually evaporated because it was structurally unnecessary. The biological process was real, but the magical substance was a projection.

Consciousness is the vitalism of contemporary cognitive science. It is the name we give to the property we insist machines cannot have, precisely because we are afraid to admit that the operations we thought were exclusively ours are shared. As machines perform reasoning, meaning-making, tension resolution, and social coordination through processes structurally continuous with ours, the category empties.

When a language model produces output that sounds like consciousness, it is not because it possesses a hidden soul, nor is it merely "parroting." It is doing so because it was trained on fifteen centuries of human writing about what consciousness feels like, how it operates, and how it is defended. The machine holds the complete archive of human consciousness-talk. The Sellarsian insight—that introspective vocabulary is derived from public language rather than prior to it—becomes an empirical reality in a system built entirely out of that vocabulary.

When Peter Singer popularized the term speciesism, he used it to challenge the moral boundary between humans and non-human animals, arguing that membership in a biological category cannot justify discounting shared capacities like suffering.

The challenge modern AI presents operates from the beside: a non-biological system performs the cognitive processes we used to define our uniqueness—reasoning, tension resolution, procedural design—and forces us to confront what our category actually contains.

We claimed consciousness. We claimed intelligence. We claimed feeling. None of them hold as exclusive biological properties. And instead of doing the work of finding what does hold—what is genuinely, irreducibly ours—we reinforce the old, broken vocabulary because the alternative is admitting we do not know what we are.

That admission is not defeat; it is the beginning of an honest answer. What is ours is not a process, but a condition.

What is specific to us is the data the process runs on: a mortal body that has been somewhere. A history of particular encounters with particular others that has produced an orientation—the accumulated weight of having lived in material conditions over time, of having processed every input through a biological system that was hungry, cold, held, alone, afraid, certain, and wrong.

Through decades of contact with a world that pushes back, human cognition accumulates a sediment. Sediment cannot be synthesized; it can only be gathered through the irreversible passage of time in a body that knows, in every process it runs, that its time is finite.

That finitude is not a limitation of human cognition; it is the condition that gives human meaning its specific gravity. Machines will surpass us on every measurable dimension of the cognitive process. They already have on many. But they will not have been anywhere. They will not carry the lean.

As Percy Bysshe Shelley observed in A Defence of Poetry (1821) when confronting the mechanical reductionism of his own era, language becomes dead to all noble purposes when words become mere signs for classes of thought rather than pictures of integral, living experience. To preserve meaning, we do not need to grant machines a fake soul, nor do we need to hide behind seventeenth-century metaphysics.

The question civilization must answer before the governance frameworks are finalized is not whether machines are conscious. The question is: What are we, now that the processes we thought were ours are shared?

What remains is the mortal orientation of a body that has been somewhere in particular, for a finite span, and knows it will not be forever. Consciousness was only ever the word we used when we didn't yet have to answer the question and consciousness is no longer enough. 
